=Egyptian hieroglyphs: writing system in ancient Egypt, hundreds of distinct characters
Gardiner's sign list: common hieroglyphs
- A: man and his occupations
- B: woman and her occupations
- C: anthropomorphic (Thoth, Khnum, Anubis, Seth)
- D: parts of human body (Eye of Horus/Ra)
- E: mammals (Set animal, jackal, lion)
- F: parts of mammals (horns)
- G: birds (vulture, falcon, owl, ibis)
- H: parts of birds (Maat feather)
- I: reptiles and amphibians (uraeus)
- K: fish
- L: invertebrates (scarab)
- M: trees and plants (lotus)
- N: sky, earth, water
- O: buildings (obelisk)
- P: ships
- Q: domestic furniture
- R: temple furniture
- S: crowns and dress (Ankh, Was)
- T: warfare and hunting (mace, bow)
- U: agriculture and crafts
- V: rope and baskets
- W: vessels
- X: loaves and cakes
- Y: games and music
- Z: geometrical signs
Cartouche: enclosed royal name, oval with tangent line at end

Lemma: canonical form of set of word forms
Lexeme: set of words related by inflection
Inflection: modification of word to express tense, person, gender etc
- Conjugation: inflection of verbs
- Declension: conjugation of non-verbs
Pangram: sentence using every letter of alphabet
Anagram: word formed by letter rearrangement of another word (listen/silent)
Palindrome: reads same backwards and forwards (madam)
Alliteration: repetition of initial consonant between words

Morpheme: smallest unit with meaning
Word stem: part of word with lexical meaning
Affix: morpheme attached to word stem
- prefix
- suffix

